Looking at this problem, I need to compute sup over all real α of the distance from the line L_α (through origin, slope α) to the nonzero integer lattice.

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains no solutions, sketches, or leaked answers. Proceeding.

**Key observation:** For any α, the distance d(L_α, Z²\{(0,0)}) equals 0, so the supremum is 0.

**Proof:**

For a point (m,n) ∈ Z², its Euclidean distance to the line L_α = {(x,y) : y = αx} is
$$\frac{|n - \alpha m|}{\sqrt{1+\alpha^2}}.$$
Therefore
$$d(L_\alpha,\,\mathbb{Z}^2\setminus\{(0,0)\}) = \inf_{(m,n)\in\mathbb{Z}^2\setminus\{(0,0)\}} \frac{|n-\alpha m|}{\sqrt{1+\alpha^2}}.$$

I show this infimum is 0 for every α ∈ ℝ.

**Case 1: α ∈ ℚ.** Write α = p/q in lowest terms with q ≥ 1. Then (q, p) ∈ ℤ², (q,p) ≠ (0,0), and p = αq, so (q,p) ∈ L_α. Hence the distance is 0.

**Case 2: α ∉ ℚ.** By Dirichlet's approximation theorem, for every integer N ≥ 1 there exist integers m, n with 1 ≤ m ≤ N and
$$|m\alpha - n| < \frac{1}{N}.$$
Given any ε > 0, choose N > 1/ε. Then there exist m, n with m ≥ 1 (so (m,n) ≠ (0,0)) and |mα − n| < ε. The point (m,n) ∈ ℤ²\{(0,0)} has distance to L_α equal to
$$\frac{|n - \alpha m|}{\sqrt{1+\alpha^2}} < \frac{\varepsilon}{\sqrt{1+\alpha^2}}.$$
Since ε > 0 was arbitrary,
$$d(L_\alpha,\,\mathbb{Z}^2\setminus\{(0,0)\}) = 0.$$

(Note: m ≥ 1 guarantees (m,n) ≠ (0,0), so the exclusion of the origin is harmless.)

**Conclusion.** Since d(L_α, ℤ²\{(0,0)}) = 0 for every α ∈ ℝ — rational α by exact incidence, irrational α by density of fractional parts {mα} in [0,1) (Dirichlet/Kronecker) — we obtain

$$\sup_{\alpha\in\mathbb{R}}\, d\!\left(L_\alpha,\,\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}\setminus\{(0,0)\}\right) = \boxed{0}.$$

